searchData={"items":[{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.audit_fingerprints","doc":"Recomputes every fingerprint from its associations and reports any row whose\nstored value has drifted from what the current algorithm produces.\n\nFingerprints are denormalised, hand-synced derived state — nothing at the DB\nlevel guarantees they match the associations — so this task is the safety net:\nrun it on demand or on a schedule to detect drift. It exits non-zero when drift\nis found, so it can gate CI.\n\n    mix rb.audit_fingerprints","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.AuditFingerprints.html"},{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.backfill_history","doc":"Records a `created` entry for every publication whose history is empty.\n\nA change is described by comparing a record against the entry before it, so\nthe first entry a record ever gets has nothing to compare against and reads as\na bare \"updated\". Publications inserted through the app open their log with a\n`created` entry and never have this problem; ones loaded straight into the\ndatabase — the original corpus, a restored dump — start with no log at all.\n\nThis gives those a starting point: the record as it stands now, attributed to\nthe system. Only publications with an empty log are touched, so a record whose\nfirst change is already recorded is left alone — its earlier state is gone and\nno baseline can honestly be invented for it.\n\nSafe to run more than once, and on a database that needs nothing.\n\n    mix rb.backfill_history","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.BackfillHistory.html"},{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.invite","doc":"Records an invitation without going through the dashboard.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited, which leaves a fresh\ndatabase with nobody who can invite anyone — this is the way in. Invite\nyourself, sign in with that address, and the role is waiting; from then on the\ndashboard does this.\n\nIt is the same mechanism the dashboard uses, so the first admin arrives the\nway everyone else does, and the log says who let them in.\n\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com admin\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com          # contributor\n\nMail is attempted and a failure is reported, not fatal — the invitation stands\neither way, and can be sent again from the dashboard.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.Invite.html"},{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.load_data","doc":"Mix task to initialize the repo using a data.csv file in the project's root directory","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.html"},{"type":"function","title":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.run/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Mix.Task.run/1 .","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.html#run/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton","doc":"RichardBurton keeps the contexts that define your domain\nand business logic.\n\nContexts are also responsible for managing your data, regardless\nif it comes from the database, an external API or others.","ref":"RichardBurton.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth","doc":"Authentication/authorization boundary. Delegates to the implementation\nconfigured under `:auth_service` (default `RichardBurton.Auth.Google`, swapped\nfor a Mox mock in tests).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.authorize/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#c:authorize/2"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.authorize/2","doc":"Returns `:ok` if `subject_id` holds `role` or one that outranks it, `:error`\notherwise. Asking for the least privilege a route needs means a role added\nabove it is admitted without revisiting the route.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#authorize/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","doc":"Authenticates an ID token, returning the identity the provider vouches for —\nthe subject and its verified email — or `:error`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims","doc":"Validates the claims of an OIDC ID token: issuer, audience, subject, expiry\nand email. Signature verification is performed separately (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Google`); this module only checks the decoded claims.\n\nThe email comes from here rather than from whoever calls the login endpoint,\nand only when the provider vouches for it: a role is granted to an address, so\nan address the caller could choose would be a role the caller could choose. A\nprovider that will not say whether an address is verified is one this cannot\naccept a login from.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.identity/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#t:identity/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.validate/4","doc":"Returns `{:ok, identity}` when every claim is valid, `:error` otherwise.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#validate/4"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf","doc":"Double-submit CSRF token: a signed, session-bound token issued as a readable\n(`http_only: false`) cookie at login and echoed back in the `rb-csrf-token`\nheader on state-changing admin requests (see `RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf`).\n\nIt is signed with the endpoint's `secret_key_base`, so it can't be forged\nwithout the server secret, and it carries the session's subject so a token\nminted for one user can't authorize another's request. Defense in depth on top\nof the `SameSite=Lax` session cookie.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.header_name/0","doc":"Name of the request header carrying the double-submit CSRF token.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#header_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.sign/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#sign/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.verify/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google","doc":"Google identity-provider implementation of the `RichardBurton.Auth` behaviour.\n\nID tokens are verified against Google's JWKS signing keys, which are cached\nand refreshed by `RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore` (using the\n`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google` provider) so that key rotation does not\nrequire a restart. The issuer, audience and expiry are all validated (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google.html"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS","doc":"Behaviour for fetching a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS): the signing keys and issuer\nused to verify ID tokens, plus the cache lifetime the provider advertises.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.result/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#t:result/0"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.fetch/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#c:fetch/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches from Google: the issuer\nfrom Google's OpenID configuration and the signing keys from its OAuth2 certs\nendpoint (respecting the certs' `Cache-Control: max-age`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches nothing.\n\nUsed under test (and in any environment where no identity provider is\nconfigured) so the key store can run without making network calls. Any token\nverification against it fails cleanly.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore","doc":"Caches a provider's JWKS signing keys and keeps them fresh so that key\nrotation does not require restarting the server.\n\nProvider-agnostic: it caches whatever any `RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS`\nimplementation returns. The implementation is configured via the `:jwks_provider`\napplication env and can be overridden per instance with the `:provider` option.\n\nKeys are refreshed:\n\n  * on a schedule, honouring the `max-age` advertised by the provider; and\n  * lazily, whenever a token presents a `kid` that is not currently cached\n    (rate-limited by `:refresh_cooldown` to avoid hammering the\n    provider with bogus `kid`s).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.fetch_key/2","doc":"Returns `{:ok, key}` for the cached key matching `kid`. On a cache miss, the\nkeys are refreshed once (rate-limited) before giving up with `:error`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#fetch_key/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.issuer/1","doc":"The issuer advertised by the provider, or nil if not fetched yet.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#issuer/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.start_link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha","doc":"Behaviour for recaptcha authorization services","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google","doc":"Google Recaptcha implementation for RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha behaviour","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session","doc":"Server-side (stateful) app sessions. Each session is a row in `auth_sessions`\nholding the authenticated user's `subject_id` and the SHA-256 hash of an opaque\ntoken. The raw token is set as the `rb-session` cookie and looked up on every\nrequest, so a session can be revoked instantly by deleting its row.\n\nLifetime is a **sliding idle timeout** (`:session_idle_timeout`) refreshed on\nuse, bounded by an **absolute cap** (`:session_max_age`) measured from creation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.cookie_name/0","doc":"Name of the httpOnly cookie carrying the raw session token.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#cookie_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.create/1","doc":"Creates a session for `subject_id`, returning the raw opaque token to set as the cookie.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#create/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.idle_timeout/0","doc":"Sliding idle timeout in seconds (`:session_idle_timeout`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#idle_timeout/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.max_age/0","doc":"Absolute session cap in seconds — also the cookie max_age (`:session_max_age`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#max_age/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke/1","doc":"Revokes the session identified by `token` (deletes its row).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke_all/1","doc":"Revokes every session for `subject_id` (sign out everywhere).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke_all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.verify/1","doc":"Verifies a raw token, returning `{:ok, subject_id}` or `:error`.\n\nRejects (and prunes) sessions past their idle timeout or absolute cap, and\nslides the idle timeout forward on a valid, active session.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Author","doc":"Schema for authors","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.flatten/1","doc":"Join a list of authors back into a comma-separated string. A non-list value\n(already flat) is returned unchanged.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Author.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Author.flatten([%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Clarice Lispector\"}])\n  \"Machado de Assis, Clarice Lispector\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Author.flatten(\"Machado de Assis\")\n  \"Machado de Assis\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.get_name/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#get_name/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.link_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#link_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.maybe_insert!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#maybe_insert!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.nest/1","doc":"Split a comma-separated authors string into nested author maps.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Author.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Author.nest(\"Machado de Assis, Clarice Lispector\")\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Clarice Lispector\"}]","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.search/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.search/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Codec","doc":"Utilities for serialization and deserialization","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens a nested map, post-processing the resulting `{key, value}` pairs with the given `post` function.\n  Lists are not traversed.\n  Keys on nested maps are regenerated prepending the key of their parent map.\n\n  Returns the flattened map with string keys.","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: [1, 2, %{a: 1}]})\n      %{\"a\" => [1, 2, %{a: 1}]}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: %{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3}})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b_a\" => 1, \"b_b\" => 2, \"b_c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.flatten(%{a: 1, b: [2, 3, %{a: 1, b: 2}], c: %{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3}})\n      %{ \"a\" => 1, \"b\" => [2, 3, %{a: 1, b: 2}], \"c_a\" => 1, \"c_b\"=> 2, \"c_c\"=> 3}","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Codec.nest/1","doc":"Automatically nests a flat map.\n  Keys are grouped by their Longest Common Prefix (LCP) to define nesting points.\n  LCP is calculated considering atomic words separated by underscore:\n\n    `aBC` and `aDE do not have a common prefix\n    `a_BC` and `a_DE` have \"a\" as common prefix\n    `a_b_BC` and `a_b_DE` have \"a_b\" as common prefix\n\n  Returns the nested map with string keys.","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Codec.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b: 2, c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c_c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c_c: 3})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c_c_c\" => 3}}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Codec.nest(%{a: 1, b_a: 1, b_b: 2, b_c_c: 3, b_c_d: 4})\n      %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => %{\"a\" => 1, \"b\" => 2, \"c\" => %{\"c\" => 3, \"d\" => 4}}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Codec.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Country","doc":"Schema for countries","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","doc":"Join a list of countries back into a comma-separated code string. A non-list\nvalue (already flat) is returned unchanged.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten([%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}])\n  \"BR, US\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten(\"BR\")\n  \"BR\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.get_code/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#get_code/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.link_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#link_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.maybe_insert!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#maybe_insert!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.names_for/1","doc":"Every name a country is searchable by: its official name, the unofficial and\ntranslated names the ISO data carries (which is where \"Reino Unido\" and\n\"Estados Unidos\" come from), and a curated supplement of abbreviations the\ndata lacks. Deduplicated, in no particular order — the search index folds them\nin, it does not display them.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#names_for/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","doc":"Split a comma-separated country-code string into nested country maps.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.nest(\"BR, US\")\n  [%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}]","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_code/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_code/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_countries/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_countries/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Email","doc":"Schema for emails","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.changeset/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.contact/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#contact/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.deliver/1","doc":"Send mail as the system itself, rather than on someone's behalf.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#deliver/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Fingerprint","doc":"Computes the fingerprints used as composite-identity keys for publications and\ntheir shared associations.\n\nA set-valued attribute (countries, publishers, translators, original authors)\nis collapsed into a single scalar so a Postgres unique constraint can enforce\nset identity — which a many-to-many relationship can't express directly.\n\nAll set fingerprints go through `of_set/1` so the delimiter is defined in one\nplace: without a separator, variable-length values collide (e.g. `[\"AnnBob\"]`\nand `[\"Ann\", \"Bob\"]` both join to `\"AnnBob\"`). The composite-record fingerprints\n(`OriginalBook`/`TranslatedBook`) instead concatenate fixed-width sub-fingerprints,\nwhich is already unambiguous, so they don't use this.","ref":"RichardBurton.Fingerprint.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Fingerprint.of_set/1","doc":"Fingerprints an order-independent set of strings.","ref":"RichardBurton.Fingerprint.html#of_set/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication","doc":"Schema for publications","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#validate/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation","doc":"A role held for an email address until whoever owns it signs in.\n\nAn admin cannot grant a role to someone who has never been here: the provider\nmints the subject id, and only on a first sign-in. So the grant waits on the\naddress instead, and the sign-in redeems it — the provider vouching for that\naddress *is* the confirmation, which is why no separate confirmation link is\nsent. Everything this rests on is therefore the identity provider's word: see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`, which refuses a token that does not carry a\nverified email.\n\nSomeone who already has an account is given the role outright; there is\nnothing left to wait for.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.admit/2","doc":"Admit a signed-in identity, if it is one this platform knows.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited. Anyone may hold a Google\naccount, so a sign-in that created one regardless would mean the set of people\nwith accounts is decided by whoever tries — and revoking an account would mean\nnothing, since signing in again would make another. Being invited is what\nadmits you; the invitation is taken up in the same breath, so the role is\nalready in hand.\n\nSomeone who has been here before is admitted on the strength of their account.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#admit/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.all/0","doc":"Every invitation, newest first — pending ones and the ones taken up.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.cancel/1","doc":"Withdraw a pending invitation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#cancel/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.get/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.invite/2","doc":"Offer `role` to an address, and say so by email.\n\nSomeone who has already signed in gets the role now — there is no sign-in left\nto wait for — and no invitation is recorded. The mail is sent after the grant\nlands, so a mail server having a bad day cannot cost someone their role; a\nfailure to send is reported so the dashboard can offer to send it again.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#invite/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.resend/1","doc":"Send a pending invitation's mail again.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#resend/1"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer","doc":"Behaviour for Mailer","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#c:send/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#send/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP","doc":"STMP implementation for RichardBurton.Mailer behaviour","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver/2","doc":"Delivers an email.\n\nIf the email is delivered it returns an `{:ok, result}` tuple. If it fails,\nreturns an `{:error, error}` tuple.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver!/2","doc":"Delivers an email, raises on error.\n\nIf the email is delivered, it returns the result. If it fails, it raises\na `DeliveryError`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.deliver_many/2","doc":"Delivers a list of emails.\n\nIt accepts a list of `%Swoosh.Email{}` as its first parameter.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#deliver_many/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.send/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.SMTP.html#send/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook","doc":"Schema for original books","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","doc":"Flatten a book to the shape a form speaks of it in: a title, and its authors\nas the one comma-separated string the field holds.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten(%RichardBurton.OriginalBook{\n  ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   authors: [%RichardBurton.Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...> })\n  %{title: \"Dom Casmurro\", authors: \"Machado de Assis\"}","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.link_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#link_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.maybe_insert!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#maybe_insert!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.preload/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#preload/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/1","doc":"The original books a term names, by their title or by one of their authors.\n\nA book is entered as a unit — a title and who wrote it — and looking it up\nshould work from whichever half the person typing happens to know. Prefixes\nfirst, so what someone is part-way through typing wins; only when nothing\nstarts that way does the search fall back to similar spellings.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication","doc":"Schema for publications","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all_deleted/0","doc":"The publications someone deleted, most recently deleted first.\n\nA record absorbed by a merge is out of the database the same way, but nobody\ndeleted it and there is no putting it back: restoring one would recreate the\nduplicate the merge collapsed, with the survivor still holding what it took.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all_deleted/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.delete/2","doc":"Soft-delete a publication: stamp `deleted_at`, so every read path hides it\nwhile its row, references, and history survive — and `restore/2` can bring\nit back. The final state rides along in the history snapshot.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.find/1","doc":"One live publication, with its associations, or `nil`.\n\nA tombstone reads as missing here exactly as it does in the index and the\nsearch — a deleted record is gone from every read path, not merely hidden from\nthe list.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#find/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert_all/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.merge/3","doc":"Collapse publications into one.\n\nThe winner keeps its identity and everything that names it — title, year, the\nwork it translates, who wrote and translated it. What the losers add is what\na record can hold more of: their countries and publishers join the winner's,\nand their sources are appended to its own. A source already recorded is not\nrecorded twice; elsewhere a publication may list the same line twice, but a\nmerge saying it twice is the merge showing, not the record meaning it.\n\nThe losers are then soft-deleted, and the whole act is recorded as one entry\non the winner naming what it took in — which is what lets it be undone as one\nact too, by `undo/3`.\n\nAnswers `{:error, :conflict}` when the merged record would collide with a\nthird publication, `{:error, :not_found}` when any of them is not here,\n`{:error, :self}` when a publication is asked to merge into itself, and\n`{:error, :no_losers}` when nothing was named to merge in.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#merge/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.preload/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#preload/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.restore/2","doc":"Bring a soft-deleted publication back into the database.\n\nOnly one that someone deleted. A record absorbed by a merge is out of the\ndatabase the same way, but it is not in the trash and does not come back on\nits own: `{:error, :absorbed}` says to take the merge apart instead.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#restore/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.undo/3","doc":"Undo one recorded change, by applying the action that compensates it.\n\nNothing is erased: the log is append-only, so the undo lands as a new entry of\nits own and is itself undoable. Eligibility is decided here rather than\ntrusted from the caller — the rule (see `History.undoable?/3`) is an invariant\nof the log, not an affordance of whichever client happens to be asking.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#undo/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.update/3","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#update/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.validate/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#validate/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec","doc":"Serialization and deserialization utilities for publications","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"Flatten a nested publication back to flat, string-keyed fields — the inverse of\n`nest/1`. Child lists are joined and the `translated_book` association is lifted\nback to top-level `authors` / `original_title` / `original_authors`. Accepts a\n`Publication` struct, a nested map, a list, or a `%{publication:, errors:}` pair.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten(%{\n  ...>   \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   \"translated_book\" => %{\n  ...>     \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n  ...>     \"original_book\" => %{\n  ...>       \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>       \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...>     }\n  ...>   }\n  ...> })\n  %{\"authors\" => \"Helen Caldwell\", \"original_authors\" => \"Machado de Assis\", \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\", \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\"}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.from_csv/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#from_csv/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.from_csv!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#from_csv!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","doc":"Nest a flat publication — a `FlatPublication` struct, a flat map, or a list of\nthem — into the shape the `Publication` changeset expects: multi-value fields\nbecome child maps, and the `translated_book`/`original_book` fields are\nre-parented under their association.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","doc":"iex> nested =\n  ...>   RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest(%{\n  ...>     \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"authors\" => \"Helen Caldwell\",\n  ...>     \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"original_authors\" => \"Machado de Assis\"\n  ...>   })\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"authors\"]\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}]\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"original_book\"][\"title\"]\n  \"Dom Casmurro\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest_authors/1","doc":"Nest a comma-separated authors string into author maps — used when building a\ntranslated book's author associations.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest_authors/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest_authors/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest_authors(\"Helen Caldwell, Gregory Rabassa\")\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Gregory Rabassa\"}]","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest_authors/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.to_csv/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#to_csv/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates","doc":"The publications that look like the same record entered twice.\n\nThe composite key blocks *exact* duplicates, so what is left are the ones it\ncannot see: a typo, an accent dropped, `St.` for `Saint`, a translator entered\nas \"R. Burton\" once and \"Richard Burton\" the next time. These read as distinct\nrecords, fragment the database and mislead every count taken from it.\n\nLikeness is trigram similarity — the same measure the author lookup uses — over\nthe fields a duplicate would agree on. Two records are candidates when their\n*translators* are alike and either their titles are alike, or the original\nbooks they render are.\n\nThe translators are what carries the question, because this is a database of\ntranslations: two people rendering one book is the subject matter, not a\nmistake. \"Dom Casmurro\" by Helen Caldwell and \"Dom Casmurro\" by John Gledson\nagree on title and original book and are still two publications. The same\ntranslator's work appearing twice is the thing worth asking about.\n\nSimilarity cannot tell two editions of one book from two records of one\nedition, and it is not meant to: it proposes, a person decides. What that\nperson rules apart is remembered (see `Distinction`), so the question is asked\nonce.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.clusters/0","doc":"The clusters worth reviewing, likeliest first.\n\nA cluster is a set of records joined by likeness: if A looks like B and B looks\nlike C, all three are one question, because merging them is one act. Pairs\nalready ruled apart are not edges, so ruling one out can split a cluster rather\nthan merely shrinking it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#clusters/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.reconsider/1","doc":"Take back a decision to tell records apart, so the review asks about them\nagain.\n\nEvery pair among the ids is forgotten, matching how `rule_apart/2` records\nthem: the answer was about the cluster, so taking it back is too.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#reconsider/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.rule_apart/2","doc":"Record that these publications are not the same record twice, so the reviewer\nis not asked about them again. Every pair among them is ruled apart: the answer\nis about the cluster the reviewer was shown.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#rule_apart/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.ruled_apart/0","doc":"The pairs someone has ruled apart, newest first, with the records themselves.\n\nA decision that cannot be seen cannot be taken back, and this is what a\nreviewer looks at to find one worth reconsidering.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#ruled_apart/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.threshold/0","doc":"How alike two records must be to be worth asking about, between 0 and 1.\n\nRead at run time from `:duplicate_threshold`, so the sensitivity can be tuned\nagainst real data — too low floods the reviewer with false clusters, too high\nmisses real duplicates — without changing any code.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#threshold/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction","doc":"One remembered \"these two are not the same\". Stored for the pair, lower id\nfirst, so the answer is the same however it is asked.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History","doc":"Append-only log of publication mutations: one immutable row per change, with\nthe action, a flattened snapshot of the record after it, the acting user, and\na per-publication version so each record's history reads as an ordered\nstream.\n\nImmutability is enforced by the database — a guard trigger rejects UPDATE and\nDELETE on the table (see the soft-delete migration) — and completeness by\nchokepoint: every mutation flows through the `Publication` context functions,\nwhich call `record/3` inside the mutating transaction, so a change and its\nhistory row commit or roll back together.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed/0","doc":"The publications that are inside another record right now.\n\nA merged record's own history is still here, but nothing in it can be undone\nwhile it is absorbed — it is the merge that holds it, and the merge that gives\nit back. The answer is the whole log's, not one stream's: the entry that holds\na record sits on the winner, so asking a loser's own history would never find\nit.\n\nOnly the ids are read back: an entry's `absorbed` map carries a whole snapshot\nper record, and none of it is needed to answer which ones are held.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed_ids/1","doc":"The ids of the publications an entry took in, or gave back.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed_ids/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed_records/1","doc":"The publications an entry took in, or gave back, ordered by id.\n\nEach one carries its own id and none of the fingerprints: those are the\nserver's bookkeeping, not part of the record being shown.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed_records/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.all/0","doc":"Every recorded mutation across the database, newest first, each diffed.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/1","doc":"Populate each entry's derived fields: what it changed relative to the previous\nversion of *its own* record, and whether it can still be undone.\n\nEntries come newest first, so a version's predecessor is the next element of\nits stream and the head is the first. Grouping by publication is what lets one\npass serve both the per-record log and the database-wide feed, where streams\ninterleave and the row above an entry usually belongs to a different\npublication. Pairing by position within the stream, rather than looking up\n`version - 1`, also holds if the version sequence ever has a gap.\n\nBoth answers need the whole stream, which is why they are computed here rather\nthan asked of one entry at a time.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/1","doc":"An update over its import — newest first, as the queries return them:\n\n    iex> alias RichardBurton.Publication.History\n    iex> [update, import] =\n    ...>   History.annotate([\n    ...>     %History{publication_id: 1, version: 2, action: \"updated\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"New\"}},\n    ...>     %History{publication_id: 1, version: 1, action: \"created\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"Old\"}}\n    ...>   ])\n    iex> update.diff.fields\n    %{\"title\" => %{from: \"Old\", to: \"New\"}}\n    iex> {update.undoable, import.diff, import.undoable}\n    {true, nil, false}\n\nThe head is always undoable; the import has nothing before it to diff against,\nand undoing it is not this entry's to offer once a later version exists.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.of/1","doc":"The ordered history of one publication, newest first, each entry diffed.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#of/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record/4","doc":"Append one history row for a mutation, inside the caller's transaction.\n\nConcurrent mutations of the same publication serialize on its row lock, so\nthe max-version read is stable; the unique index on (publication_id, version)\nturns any residual race into a loud error instead of silent corruption.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.reverted_snapshot/3","doc":"The state undoing an update produces: the record as it stands now, with\nexactly the fields that update changed put back to their pre-change values.\nFor a record's latest update that equals its previous snapshot; for an older\none, later changes to other fields survive.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#reverted_snapshot/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","doc":"The flattened state stored with a history entry: the record as it stands,\nwith its associations collapsed to the flat fields the client speaks in.\n\nComparing two of these is how callers tell an edit that changed something\nfrom one that did not — the changeset cannot answer that, because references\nare replaced wholesale on every save and so always look dirty.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","doc":"Associations arrive flattened, as they are stored:\n\n    iex> alias RichardBurton.{Author, Country, OriginalBook, Publication, Publisher, TranslatedBook}\n    iex> publication = %Publication{\n    ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>   year: 1953,\n    ...>   countries: [%Country{code: \"US\"}],\n    ...>   publishers: [%Publisher{name: \"Noonday Press\"}],\n    ...>   references: [],\n    ...>   translated_book: %TranslatedBook{\n    ...>     authors: [%Author{name: \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n    ...>     original_book: %OriginalBook{\n    ...>       title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>       authors: [%Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n    ...>     }\n    ...>   }\n    ...> }\n    iex> snapshot = Publication.History.snapshot(publication)\n    iex> {snapshot[:title], snapshot[:authors], snapshot[:countries]}\n    {\"Dom Casmurro\", \"Helen Caldwell\", \"US\"}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.system_actor/0","doc":"Actor attributed to mutations that happen outside a request.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#system_actor/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.undoable?/3","doc":"Whether undoing this entry would yield \"as if it had never happened, with\neverything after it preserved\":\n\n  - a record's latest entry, always — the compensating action applies\n    directly: restore a delete, delete an import or a restore, revert an\n    update;\n  - an older *update*, only while the record is live and every field it\n    changed still holds the value it set — the revert then touches exactly\n    those fields and leaves later edits to others alone;\n  - never an older delete (a later restore already negated it) nor an older\n    import or restore (compensating those would discard the edits that\n    followed);\n  - a merge or an un-merge, only as the latest entry, by that same rule —\n    each is one act over several records, so compensating it means taking the\n    whole thing back: the record that survived gives up what it absorbed, and\n    the records that left come back. What followed an older one was written\n    against the merged record.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#undoable?/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index","doc":"Full-text search over the publication index.\n\nA search runs in one of two modes. A plain term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into\nalternatives, any of which may match; within an alternative the words are\nmatched each on its own (as a prefix, or fuzzily if nothing matches as typed)\nand combined with AND, so each word narrows the result. A term that quotes a\nphrase or negates a word is instead passed to Postgres verbatim, exactly as\nwritten. Either way the match runs against a `tsvector` built with accents\nfolded, so a term is folded the same way before it is compared.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all_order/0","doc":"The ids of the whole database, in the order it is listed — by title, then id.\nThe counterpart of `search_order/1` for a reader browsing rather than searching.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all_order/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count_header/0","doc":"Name of the response header carrying the index's total count.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count_header/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.details/3","doc":"The full rows for the given ids, in that order — one stretch of an ordering\n`search_order/1` or `all_order/0` handed back. A `nil` term is a plain\nlisting; a term and the words it matched on (as `search_order/1` returned\nthem) are carried so a row matched only by its references still shows which —\nand shows it without resolving the term over again.\n\nAn id no longer in the database is simply left out, which is how a deletion\nsince the order was fixed shows up: a gap, never a shifted or repeated row.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#details/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.per_page/0","doc":"How many publications a page holds.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#per_page/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/2","doc":"The publications a term matches, and the indexed words it matched on.\n\nThe term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into alternatives, and a publication\nmatches if it satisfies any of them. Within an alternative the words are\ncombined with AND, so each word narrows: a publication must match every word\nof the alternative. That is why searching a full title returns just that\ntitle, while \"one title or another\" returns both. For a single word, matching\nany of the indexed words it could be is enough, so a half-typed word matches\neverything it might still become. The term is tried as typed first, and only\nif that matches nothing is it retried fuzzily, dropping a word that matches\nnothing rather than emptying the result.\n\nUnbounded, for the caller that needs all of it at once — the CSV export is a\ndownload of the database, not a page of it. A reader takes it a page at a time\nthrough `search_order/1` and `details/3` instead.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_keywords/1","doc":"The indexed words a search term matches.\n\nThe term is split into words and each word is matched on its own, because\nboth kinds of match only work word by word: a prefix is the start of a single\nword, and trigram similarity between a word and a whole phrase drops toward\nzero as the phrase grows. Matching a full title as one string would find\nnothing. A word that matches no indexed word is simply left out, so one\nunmatched word does not make the whole search return empty.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_keywords/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_keywords/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_keywords/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_order/1","doc":"The whole ordering a search resolves to — the ids of every publication it\nmatches, in the order they are to be read — and the words it matched on, or\n`:none` when nothing in the index answers at all.\n\nThe order is settled here, once. A reader then pages through it by id (see\n`details/3`) and sees a stable list, because the order was fixed the moment\nthe search ran: rows cannot shift, skip or repeat as the database changes\nunderneath the scroll.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_order/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.without_references/0","doc":"Publications with no provenance, ordered by id — the stable queue the references\nbackfill steps through.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#without_references/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher","doc":"Owns the read model: write paths call `refresh/0` once per logical operation\n(a bulk insert, an update), and the refresher rebuilds the `flat_publications`,\n`search_documents` and `search_keywords` materialized views — the flattened\nrows the index lists, and the search documents and keywords stacked on them.\n\nThe strategy comes from `config :richard_burton, :search_index_refresh`:\n\n  * `{:debounced, ms}` (default — dev and prod): signals coalesce; the views\n    rebuild once after a quiet window, outside any transaction, with\n    `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY` so searches never block and\n    writes never wait. Search lags a write by up to the window — the\n    documented trade-off.\n  * `:sync_concurrent` (e2e): rebuild inline before `refresh/0` returns —\n    deterministic for browser tests while exercising the same CONCURRENTLY\n    path production runs.\n  * `:sync` (test): rebuild inline with a plain `REFRESH` — the ExUnit\n    sandbox wraps tests in transactions, where CONCURRENTLY is not allowed.\n\nIn debounced mode the refresher also rebuilds once on boot, covering signals\nlost to a restart and data seeded while the app was down.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.refresh/0","doc":"Signal that the index is stale. Returns after the rebuild in sync modes.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#refresh/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.start_link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument","doc":"Schema for searchable documents","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchKeyword","doc":"Schema for searchable documents","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchKeyword.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher","doc":"Schema for publishers","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten/1","doc":"Join a list of publishers back into a comma-separated string. A non-list value\n(already flat) is returned unchanged.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten([%{\"name\" => \"Noonday Press\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Penguin\"}])\n  \"Noonday Press, Penguin\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten(\"Noonday Press\")\n  \"Noonday Press\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.get_name/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#get_name/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.link_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#link_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.maybe_insert!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#maybe_insert!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.nest/1","doc":"Split a comma-separated publishers string into nested publisher maps.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publisher.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publisher.nest(\"Noonday Press, Penguin\")\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Noonday Press\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Penguin\"}]","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.search/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.search/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Reference","doc":"Schema for publication references: free-text provenance entries that record how\na publication's data was collected, so a reader can verify the claim. Owned by a\nsingle publication (deleted with it), ordered, and edited inline via the\npublication update path — never shared or deduplicated across publications.","ref":"RichardBurton.Reference.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Reference.flatten/1","doc":"Reverse of `nest/1`: an ordered list of the reference strings. Accepts loaded\nchild structs (ordered by `position`) or an already-flat list; anything else\n(nil, `%NotLoaded{}`) flattens to an empty list.","ref":"RichardBurton.Reference.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Reference.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Reference.flatten([\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Reference{content: \"Second\", position: 1},\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Reference{content: \"First\", position: 0}\n  ...> ])\n  [\"First\", \"Second\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Reference.flatten(nil)\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Reference.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Reference.nest/1","doc":"Turn the flat wire shape (an ordered list of free-text strings) into the nested\nchild maps `cast_assoc(:references)` expects, assigning `position` from order.\nBlank rows are dropped so an empty editor row doesn't fail validation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Reference.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Reference.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Reference.nest([\"First source\", \"Second source\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First source\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second source\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Reference.nest([\"A source\", \"  \"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"A source\", \"position\" => 0}]\n\n  A blank in the middle is dropped and positions stay contiguous — the entry\n  after it is `1`, not `2`:\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Reference.nest([\"First\", \"\", \"Second\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Reference.nest(nil)\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Reference.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Release","doc":"Used for executing DB release tasks when run in production without Mix\ninstalled.","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Release.migrate/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#migrate/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Release.rollback/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#rollback/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Repo","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/4 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all_by/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all_by/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all_by/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checked_out?/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checked_out?/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checked_out?/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checkout/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checkout/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checkout/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.child_spec/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.config/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.config/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#config/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.default_options/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.default_options/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#default_options/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete_all/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete_all/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.disconnect_all/2","doc":"Forces all connections in the repo pool to disconnect within the given interval.\n\nOnce this function is called, the pool will disconnect all of its connections\nas they are checked in or as they are pinged. Checked in and idle connections\nwill be randomly disconnected within the given time interval.\n\nIf the connection has a backoff configured (which is the case by default),\ndisconnecting means an attempt at a new connection will be done immediately\nafter, without starting a new process for each connection. However, if backoff\nhas been disabled, the connection process will terminate. In such cases,\ndisconnecting all connections may cause the pool supervisor to restart\ndepending on the max_restarts/max_seconds configuration of the pool,\nso you will want to set those carefully.\n\nIf you need connections to be restarted periodically, set the `:max_lifetime`\noption in your repository configuration instead.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#disconnect_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.exists?/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.exists?/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#exists?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"Executes an EXPLAIN statement or similar for the given query according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"# Postgres\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.explain(Repo, :all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    # MySQL\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\")) |> IO.puts()\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    | id | select_type | table | partitions | type | possible_keys | key  | key_len | ref  | rows | filtered | Extra       |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    |  1 | SIMPLE      | p0    | NULL       | ALL  | NULL          | NULL | NULL    | NULL |    1 |    100.0 | Using where |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n\n    # Shared opts\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post, analyze: true, timeout: 20_000)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=443) (actual time=0.013..0.013 rows=0 loops=1)\\nPlanning Time: 0.031 ms\\nExecution Time: 0.021 ms\"\n\nIt's safe to execute it for updates and deletes, no data change will be committed:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(Repo, :update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: \"new title\"]]))\n    \"Update on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\\n  ->  Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\"\n\nThis function is also available under the repository with name `explain`:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\"))\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\\n  Filter: ((title)::text = 'title'::text)\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"Built-in adapters support passing `opts` to the EXPLAIN statement according to the following:\n\nAdapter          | Supported opts\n---------------- | --------------\nPostgrex         | `analyze`, `verbose`, `costs`, `settings`, `buffers`, `timing`, `summary`, `format`, `plan`, `wrap_in_transaction`\nMyXQL            | `format`, `wrap_in_transaction`\n\nAll options except `format` are boolean valued and default to `false`.\n\nThe allowed `format` values are `:map`, `:yaml`, and `:text`:\n  * `:map` is the deserialized JSON encoding.\n  * `:yaml` and `:text` return the result as a string.\n\nThe built-in adapters support the following formats:\n  * Postgrex: `:map`, `:yaml` and `:text`\n  * MyXQL: `:map` and `:text`\n\nThe `wrap_in_transaction` option is a boolean that controls whether the command is run inside of a\ntransaction that is rolled back. This is useful when, for example, you'd like to use `analyze: true`\non an update or delete query without modifying data. Defaults to `true`.\n\nThe `:plan` option in Postgrex can take the values `:custom` or `:fallback_generic`. When `:custom`\nis specified, the explain plan generated will consider the specific values of the query parameters\nthat are supplied. When using `:fallback_generic`, the specific values of the query parameters will\nbe ignored. `:fallback_generic` does not use PostgreSQL's built-in support for a generic explain\nplan (available as of PostgreSQL 16), but instead uses a special implementation that works for PostgreSQL\nversions 12 and above. Defaults to `:custom`.\n\nAny other value passed to `opts` will be forwarded to the underlying adapter query function, including\nshared Repo options such as `:timeout`. Non built-in adapters may have specific behaviour and you should\nconsult their documentation for more details.\n\nFor version compatibility, please check your database's documentation:\n\n  * _Postgrex_: [PostgreSQL doc](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-explain.html).\n  * _MyXQL_: [MySQL doc](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/explain.html).","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_dynamic_repo/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_unique_key_values/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_unique_key_values/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.in_transaction?/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.in_transaction?/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#in_transaction?/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_all/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_all/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_all/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.load/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.load/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#load/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.maybe_insert!/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#maybe_insert!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.preload/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.preload/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#preload/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_query/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_query/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_query/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_transaction/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_transaction/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_transaction/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#put_dynamic_repo/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query.\n\nIf the query was successful, it will return an `:ok` tuple containing\na map with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query(\"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query!/3","doc":"Same as `query/3` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query that returns multiple results on the given repo.\n\nIn case of success, it must return an `:ok` tuple containing a list of\nmaps with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query_many(\"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query_many(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many!/3","doc":"Same as `query_many/4` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.rollback/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.rollback/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#rollback/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.start_link/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.start_link/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stop/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stop/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stop/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stream/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stream/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stream/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","doc":"Converts the given query to SQL according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","doc":"The examples below are meant for reference. Each adapter will\nreturn a different result:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:all, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: ^\"hello\"]]))\n    {\"UPDATE posts AS p SET title = $1\", [\"hello\"]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.to_sql(:all, MyRepo, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transact/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transact/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transact/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transaction/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transaction/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transaction/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update_all/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update_all/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update_all/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook","doc":"Schema for translated books","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.link_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#link_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.maybe_insert!/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#maybe_insert!/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.preload/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html#preload/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.User","doc":"Schema for users","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.all/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.at_least?/2","doc":"Whether `role` is `required`, or outranks it.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#at_least?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.cast_role/1","doc":"The role named by `role`, given as a string or an atom.\n\nNames arrive as strings from request params, the console and the dev\nprovider; only this knows which strings are roles.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#cast_role/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.delete/2","doc":"Revoke a user's access, on behalf of `actor` — the subject id of whoever is\nasking, or `nil` from the console.\n\nThe account goes, and with it the sessions that were signed in as them —\notherwise a revoked user keeps the run of the place until their cookie happens\nto expire. Removing your own account, or the last admin's, is refused as it is\nin `set_role/3`.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_email/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_email/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_id/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_id/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.insert/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#insert/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.roles/0","doc":"The roles, least privileged first. A role admits everything the ones before it\nadmit: a contributor adds and corrects publications, an admin also decides who may.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#roles/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.set_role/3","doc":"Give a user a role, on behalf of `actor` — the subject id of whoever is\nasking, or `nil` from the console.\n\nTwo changes are refused: your own role, and the one that would leave the\ndatabase without an admin. Answers `{:error, :self}` or `{:error, :last_admin}`\nrespectively, and `{:error, :invalid_role}` for a role that is not one.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#set_role/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Util","doc":"Utilities for general purpose","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Util.create_fingerprint/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#create_fingerprint/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Util.deep_merge_maps/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#deep_merge_maps/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","doc":"Given an enumerable with atom keys, return the enumerable with string keys instead.\n  Works for keyword lists, key-value tuples, maps and structs.\n  When an ordinary list is given, it stringify the keys of its elements.\n  Does not traverse structures other than ordinary lists.","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{})\n      %{}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{\"key\" => \"value\"})\n      %{\"key\" => \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({\"key\", \"value\"})\n      {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([])\n      []\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, \"value\"})\n      {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, :value})\n      {\"key\", :value}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key1: \"value\", key2: 1234])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key: :value])\n      [{\"key\", :value}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {:key2, 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key1: \"value\", key2: 1234})\n      %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: :value})\n      %{\"key\" => :value}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{:key1 => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234})\n      %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, %{key2: 1234}])\n      [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, %{\"key2\" => 1234}]\n\n      iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: {:key, \"value\"}})\n      %{\"key\" => {:key, \"value\"}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Validation","doc":"Utilities for data validation","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.get_errors/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#get_errors/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.validate/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#validate/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.validate_no_duplicates/4","doc":"Rejects repeated entries in a cast association, comparing each child by\n`key`. Without this a publication carrying the same country, publisher or\nauthor twice reaches the join tables' unique indexes and raises there,\nturning a data-entry slip into a 500.\n\nErrors are reported under `field` unless `:as` names another key. Nested\nerrors collapse to their innermost map, so an original book's `authors`\nwould otherwise arrive indistinguishable from the translators' — `:as` puts\nit under the flat name the client knows it by.","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#validate_no_duplicates/4"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb","doc":"The entrypoint for defining your web interface, such\nas controllers, views, channels and so on.\n\nThis can be used in your application as:\n\n    use RichardBurtonWeb, :controller\n    use RichardBurtonWeb, :view\n\nThe definitions below will be executed for every view,\ncontroller, etc, so keep them short and clean, focused\non imports, uses and aliases.\n\nDo NOT define functions inside the quoted expressions\nbelow. Instead, define any helper function in modules\nand import those modules here.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html"},{"type":"macro","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.__using__/1","doc":"When used, dispatch to the appropriate controller/view/etc.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#__using__/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.channel/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#channel/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.controller/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#controller/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.router/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#router/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.view/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#view/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.index/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.AuthorController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController","doc":"Dev/test-only credentials provider. Mints an `rb-session` (plus the readable\n`csrf-token`) without the Google OAuth handshake, so the admin UI can be\nexercised locally.\n\nA role can be asked for — `%{\"role\" => \"contributor\"}` — so the surfaces an\nadmin sees and a contributor does not can be exercised as each.\n\nKept out of production two ways: this file lives outside `lib/`, on a compile\npath only added for `:dev`, `:test` and `:e2e` (see `elixirc_paths` in\n`mix.exs`), so the\nmodule is not compiled into a prod release at all; and the route is wired only\nwhen `Mix.env() in [:dev, :test, :e2e]` (see the router).","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.create/2","doc":"Upserts the dev user for the asked-for role and sets its session cookies.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController","doc":"Resets the database between Playwright tests: truncates every table, restarts\nidentity sequences, and refreshes the search index. Mounted only in the :e2e\nenvironment (see the router). Each worker owns its own database, so a reset\nonly clears that worker's data — this is what keeps parallel workers isolated.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.create/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.contact/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html#contact/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from!/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.call/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.call/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns the child specification to start the endpoint\nunder a supervision tree.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config/2","doc":"Returns the endpoint configuration for `key`\n\nReturns `default` if the key does not exist.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config_change/2","doc":"Reloads the configuration given the application environment changes.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config_change/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.host/0","doc":"Returns the host for the given endpoint.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#host/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.init/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.init/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.path/1","doc":"Generates the path information when routing to this endpoint.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#path/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.script_name/0","doc":"Generates the script name.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#script_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.server_info/1","doc":"Returns the address and port that the server is running on","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#server_info/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the endpoint supervision tree.\n\nAll other options are merged into the endpoint configuration.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_integrity/1","doc":"Generates a base64-encoded cryptographic hash (sha512) to a static file\nin `priv/static`. Meant to be used for Subresource Integrity with CDNs.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_integrity/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_lookup/1","doc":"Returns a two item tuple with the first item being the `static_path`\nand the second item being the `static_integrity`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_lookup/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_path/1","doc":"Generates a route to a static file in `priv/static`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_path/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_url/0","doc":"Generates the static URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:static_url` to generate\nsuch. It falls back to `:url` if `:static_url` is not set.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_url/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.struct_url/0","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL but as a `URI` struct.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.\nUseful for manipulating the URL data and passing it to\nURL helpers.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#struct_url/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.subscribe/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.subscribe/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#subscribe/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#unsubscribe/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.url/0","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#url/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorHelpers","doc":"Conveniences for translating and building error messages.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorHelpers.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.__resource__/0","doc":"The resource name, as an atom, for this view","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#__resource__/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.render/2","doc":"Renders the given template locally.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#render/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.template_not_found/2","doc":"Callback invoked when no template is found.\nBy default it raises but can be customized\nto render a particular template.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorView.html#template_not_found/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.create/2","doc":"Offer a role to an address.\n\nAnswers with what happened, because the three outcomes are genuinely\ndifferent: someone already here was granted the role, someone new has an\ninvitation waiting, or they have one waiting that could not be mailed to them.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.delete/2","doc":"Withdraw a pending invitation.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.index/2","doc":"Every invitation — the ones waiting, and the ones taken up.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.resend/2","doc":"Send a pending invitation's mail again.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#resend/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.index/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer","doc":"Authenticates a request via an identity-provider token in the\n`Authorization: Bearer` header (see `RichardBurton.Auth`) and assigns\n`:subject_id` and `:email`. Used by the login endpoints that exchange the\nprovider token for a session.\n\nThe email is assigned from the verified token, not read from the request, so\nwhat the account is keyed on is the provider's word rather than the caller's.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.call/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.init/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie","doc":"Authenticates a request via the app's own `rb-session` cookie\n(see `RichardBurton.Auth.Session`) and assigns `:subject_id`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.call/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.init/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize","doc":"Authorizes the request when the authenticated subject (assigned by an\n`Authenticate` plug) holds the given role, or one that outranks it.\n\n    plug RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize, role: :contributor\n\nA route asks for the least it needs, so a role added above that one is\nadmitted without every route being revisited. The role is re-read per request,\nso granting or revoking takes effect on the next one rather than the next\nsign-in.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.call/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.init/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha","doc":"Plug for recaptcha verification","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.call/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.init/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf","doc":"Double-submit CSRF check for state-changing admin requests. Safe methods\n(GET/HEAD/OPTIONS) pass through untouched. For unsafe methods it requires an\n`rb-csrf-token` header carrying a valid `RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf` token whose\nsubject matches the authenticated session (assigned by `Authenticate.Cookie`,\nwhich must run first). Defense in depth on top of the `SameSite=Lax` cookie.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.call/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.init/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html#init/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.create_all/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#create_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.delete/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinctions/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinctions/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinguish/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinguish/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.duplicates/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#duplicates/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.export/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#export/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.history/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#history/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index_deleted/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index_deleted/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.merge/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#merge/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.reconsider/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#reconsider/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.restore/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#restore/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.show/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#show/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.undo/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#undo/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.update/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.validate/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#validate/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.index/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublisherController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.api/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#api/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authenticate_bearer/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authenticate_bearer/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_admin/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_admin/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_contributor/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_contributor/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_recaptcha/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_recaptcha/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.call/2","doc":"Callback invoked by Plug on every request.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.files/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#files/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.formatted_routes/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.formatted_routes/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#formatted_routes/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.init/1","doc":"Callback required by Plug that initializes the router\nfor serving web requests.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.verified_route?/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.verified_route?/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#verified_route?/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController","doc":"Exchanges a verified identity-provider token for the app's own session cookie.\n\nThe `Authenticate` plug has already verified the incoming provider token and\nassigned the identity it vouches for; this action upserts the user and sets\nthe `rb-session` cookie so subsequent requests authenticate without forwarding\nthe provider token.\n\nThe account is keyed on the assigned subject and email — never on the request\nbody, which the caller controls and which an invitation would otherwise let\nthem turn into a role.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.create/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.delete/2","doc":"Clears the `rb-session` cookie so the browser is signed out. The cookie is\nhttp-only, so only a server response can expire it; no auth is required since\nclearing one's own cookie is harmless.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.delete/2","doc":"Revoke someone's access, and the sessions signed in as them.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.index/2","doc":"Everyone with access, and what they may do.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.me/2","doc":"Returns the current user for a valid `rb-session` cookie, or `null`. Public\n(reads the cookie itself) so the SPA can poll auth state without a 401.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#me/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.update/2","doc":"Change what a user may do.\n\nThe last admin cannot be demoted: the platform would be left with nobody who\ncan grant access, and no way back except the database.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#update/2"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.3"}}